we're joined now by the conservative mp  for rutland and melton alicia kearns  thank you so much for being with us  we were just talking to grant shapster  about uh refugees he's hoping to get  some ukrainian family of ukrainian  family into his home and i know that  you've been  involved in pairing ukrainian families  to people in your constituency and also  fellow mps  how have you found honestly how have you  found the system to navigate  so  the frustration is that you have to know  ukrainians to be able to match with them  uh the good news is because i have the  privilege of working with the most  amazing group of ukrainian mps they've  matched me with i think i think so far  i've matched 26 uh ukrainian families to  i think 20 homes in my constituency and  then another five or six families to uh  british mps who are willing to take  people into their homes and it has been  a mixed picture i have one visa that was  applied for on the monday and came  through by the tuesday afternoon um i've  got another visa that has been in the  system now for 10 days um  the frustration is obviously we still  need to go faster and further in terms  of making sure people can apply in their  own language and that we get these  things done quickly but when i speak to  ukrainian mps actually they're saying we  want you to do security checks because  we want this to work and there is a  difference to other schemes and that we  are inviting people into british  people's homes so there is a there is a  duty of care there to make sure this is  going to work um but so far we've got a  number of people arriving tomorrow um  and i think we're all very much looking  forward to working them into our homes  i do  totally recognize the duty of care when  people are coming into individuals homes  from both sides from the uk side but  also from the ukrainian family side as  well  but should the visa process be at least  changed in some way to allow people to  get out of ukraine as quickly as they  can and then some of the bureaucracy can  be done when they're here in the united  kingdom  we all want this to move as quickly as  possible and i think the scenes we're  seeing from bucca and from mario paul  make clear that there are war crimes  taking place and we want people out as  soon as possible the vast majority of  ukrainians that i've been matching with  my constituents are actually already out  of ukraine so they are applying from  hungary or from germany or from poland  and places like that so  do you  know that ukrainians are getting out  safely and now we just need to get them  to the uk  okay a couple of issues with the signal  there but i think it should be okay now  so we're going to keep going and we were  talking about the energy strategy as  well and this of course links into the  cost of living a crisis has been stories  in the newspapers today about up to  seven new nuclear stations and it was  interesting listening to grant shapso he  was pretty firm on the idea of onshore  wind saying it was an eyesore that's not  something that he could support and do  you think that the government is right  to be moving away from onshore wind  so i think the offshore wind is the  greatest opportunity that we have in  terms of energy and i know that the  prime minister's spoken before about  being the saudi arabia of wind you know  we have the celtic sea i think that's  where we should be investing the  majority of our wind focus uh in terms  of onshore wind i'm probably one of the  only people in the country that thinks  that uh onshore wind is actually quite  beautiful in its own way uh but i  recognize that i don't probably  represent the majority there so i think  offshore wind should absolutely be our  priority and that also allows us to move  away from our reliance on these  dangerous solar farm supply chains where  too many of the companies are bound up  in the genocide we're seeing of the  uyghur  at the same time though it's probably  right to declare your own interest i  mean you've campaigned to stop a solar  farm in your constituency  is this not part of the issue though  that you know  if we are going to get energy security  and an energy supply that is going to  heat people's homes and securely  someone's going to have to live near  solar farms onshore wind farms nuclear  power stations as well i mean how are  you going to square that  we already have solar plants in rutland  the mountain and we will have more solar  plants in rotten debt in the future but  what i won't accept is having a company  such as canadian solar who have been  they've had some of their suppliers  sanctioned by the us government they've  had four shipments of their solar plants  to the us taken off them because they  have been proven now including by report  by the university of sheffield to be  complicit in the genocide of the weaker  people i will not have uh bloodstained  solar panels essentially pollute our  environment there are alternatives to  polysilicon that can go into solar  panels there's a great piece of work  done at loughborough university there  are companies that are producing these  solar panels now without polysilicon so  we can clean up our supply chains we can  stop using the cheap uyghur slave labor  that is making the vast majority of  solar panels around the country in the  world at the moment but that's a choice  in the decision we have to make so i'm  not saying no to solar quite the  opposite i'm saying we want cleaner  solar and solar that isn't cheap for us  because it's built on the blood and  slave labor of others  now i want to talk to you a bit about  maternity care in the united kingdom you  know  and just sort of speaking frankly for a  minute you know on a sunday show you're  often looking to throw forward to  stories that are happening next week  like the energy security strategy  and but i actually want to just take a  moment and speak about a story that i  found extremely passionately affecting  this week  and it is this  story that 201 babies and nine mothers  died as a result of failings at the  shuri's b and telford nhs trust i mean  we're talking about the deaths of more  than 200  babies and i know that you're someone  who's spoken out about maternity care  before  can we really say the uk is a safe place  for women to give birth in  i think  i think that reports  the anger and the heartbreak that i have  felt and clearly you have felt and so  many ass has felt  i have to say that as an mp over the  last two and a half years i have sadly  had to conclude that nhs bureaucracies  are inherently and systematically sexist  there is a constant expectation that  women's services and care can be done on  the cheap or that because women have  given birth for generations and  generations they don't deserve the  support they need and my mother saw me  speak out in parliament and actually she  said to me that she remembered when she  was giving birth to my brother and she  asked for a c-section no i like she  asked for an epidural and because she'd  been in labor for so many days and so  many in so much pain  and she remembers being screamed at by  the consultant and told you think you're  special you're not special women do this  every day  again i shared my own experience of  having a c-section i ended up being  taken to the operating theatre twice  um because they weren't willing to give  me the c-section that i needed when i'd  been in labor 36 hours and my son had  already almost died once because of this  obsession with stats and low c-section  rates and also this idea that there was  a normal birth there is no such thing as  the births that we see in movies some  women have amazing birth stories that  these should not be expected of all  women um and i do worry that we have  seen it be very unsafe over the last two  years as we saw the campaign that you  and i spoke about a lot about partners  being locked up the room that made women  less safe so i think we have a way to go  but i think anyone who's watching this  is about to go into labor  know that our hospitals are safe but you  need to know that you have every right  to challenge the system and to say at  any point wait know why what are the  alternatives and why are you doing this  it is your body and you have a right to  challenge and demand better and demand  what you need to keep you safe  i think this is exactly it isn't it it's  this feeling that  what happened at sheridan's health is  the the worst example of when the system  goes wrong but every woman and many  women listening to this program  all their mothers or their sisters or  their friends will have their own  stories of birth not going in the way  that they wanted of losing control of  the birth and of not feeling safe or  supported and i think it as you say it  goes back to that thing about women  being listened to  what needs to change going forward and  what can the government do to try and  make the situation better or less sexist  as you say  i think sajid is exactly the man to deal  with this he cares deeply  about fighting for women's rights he  cares deeply about families and  supporting them and i know that he has  been personally found it very hard  seeing the conclusions of this report  and how much women and families i mean  people lives were lost and that  heartache and pain that people went  through is something that they will  still be living with today  and so i have absolute confidence that  sergeant is going to make sure maternity  system gets what it needs he's given it  the biggest investment of funds that i  think it's ever seen  and i believe truly that if there is a  health secretary that will fight for it  it is sadjid and he will make sure that  women's voices are listened to and he  has committed to looking around the  country to make sure that we don't see  something like this again  okay thank you for talking so openly  about um  about what happened and your own  experiences as well and just finally  you've been um on the uh gay conversion  therapy ban i was just keen to try and  unpick what exactly happened  this week first the prime minister said  it was gonna be banned then he  backtracked then he was contacted by 60  mps i believe including yourself um and  then he said it would be bad can you  just unpick what exactly went on because  it looks pretty shambolic from someone  on the outside  um look obviously i don't work in number  10 i don't know exactly what happened  but i do know that when i got elected i  went to the prime minister and said we  need to ban conversion therapy it is  wrong it tells every gay and trans  person in this country that they don't  deserve to be loved that they have some  sort of pathology that can be cured and  that is wrong and he was he was really  surprised that it was still legal  because when he was mayor of london he  banned the advertising conversion  therapy on the tube because he  recognized how wrong and dangerous it  was so in all my conversations with him  he has backed this ban entirely it was  in the last queen speech um and then yes  on friday we saw this leaked document  that suggests the government was going  to drop the ban um and i think  colleagues very rightfully made clear to  the prime minister and to everyone else  within the system how strongly they felt  that this cannot continue within our  country we will not allow gay and trans  people to feel attacked threatened and  unsafe in our country by allowing this  legislation to well essentially this  appalling quackery and fraudulent  activity to continue in our country  and the good thing now is that the  promise has been cleared the ban will go  forward um but for me now it's about  making sure it is the right ban and that  crucially we don't say that trans people  will be excluded from this ban because  they are vulnerable  and when people want to criticize this  bill i would say very clearly if you are  arguing that there's not enough critical  conversations taken with young people  who are questioning their gender  identity then you should back this bill  because this bill says if you are  questioning your sexuality or your  gender identity  then when you want to have these  challenging critical and safe  conversations they should take place  with a regulated and accredited  individual not a quack not a woke hippie  around the corner someone who will  genuinely work with you and support you  through this process that should be what  we want for all of our children and we  should want to send a very clear message  that everyone in this country deserves  to be loved and that being who you are  is never a pathology  okay thank you for being on the program  today we are out of time thank you

